  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up loss bowl, loss = (4x² + z²)/2, with simulated batch noise. No real model or data is used.",
    "The bowl is drawn at 0.5 × loss and is steeper along x. Without noise, each x step multiplies x by (1 − 4 × learning rate), so it overshoots above 0.25 and diverges above 0.5. The z direction is stable for every rate up to 1.",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 1.5 × √((64 − b)/(63 b)) per setting. That is 1.5 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at batch 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are 2 × the step they show (learning rate × slope estimate) and point the way the ball moves. Orange uses the noisy mini-batch slope, grey the full-data slope. Arrows hide when a run ends, and the step length then reads 0.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is diverged when the loss goes past 8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl (|x| or |z| above 2). The ball and trail are capped at the bowl's edge, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed in one continuing sequence. A new run uses the next values, and Reset replays the sequence from the start. While running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}